(window.LADDER_CARDS["16a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16a1.1","q":"What is a stand-in letter, and why is ∫ₛˣ f(x) dx not written?","a":"the letter after d in an integral, which names no number outside it and can be swapped for any letter not in use; with x as the end too, one letter would do two jobs at once","w":["the letter naming the upper end; ∫ₛˣ f(x) dx is not written because s is missing","a letter that names the integral's value; it is not written because x is too common","the letter f; it is not written because integrals have no ends"],"why":"A name names one thing at a time, so the inside letter is changed: ∫ₛˣ f(t) dt. ∫ₚ^q f(t) dt and ∫ₚ^q f(z) dz are the same number."},
 {"u":"16a2.1","q":"What is the area function of f from s, and what does the split-in-any-order technique say?","a":"the function F with F(x) = ∫ₛˣ f(t) dt; for any three points p, q, r of the interval, in any order, the integral from p to q is the integral from p to r plus the integral from r to q","w":["the function f itself; the integral splits only when p < r < q","the slope of f from s; the integral from p to q is the product of the two parts","the greatest value of f up to x; the integral cannot be split"],"why":"F is 0 at s and counts negative for x < s or where f is below the axis. 15e's two conventions make the split work in every order."},
 {"u":"16a3.1","q":"What does the area-bound technique say, and what does area-never-jumps say about F where f jumps?","a":"if |f| ≤ M on [p, q] then |∫ₚ^q f| ≤ M(q − p); the area function is continuous wherever f is integrable, with |F(y) − F(x)| ≤ M|y − x|, so F does not jump where f does","w":["the integral is at least M(q − p); F jumps wherever f jumps","the integral equals M; F has no value where f jumps","the integral is at most M; F is constant where f jumps"],"why":"F(y) − F(x) = ∫ₓʸ f, and area-bound holds it within M(y − x). f may jump; F cannot."},
 {"u":"16a4.1","q":"What happens at 0 to the area function of the step u from 0?","a":"it does not jump: it is 0 up to 0 and x after, with a joint at 0, arriving at the same height and leaving at a different rate","w":["it jumps from 0 to 1","it has slope ½ at 0","it has no value at 0"],"why":"Going from f to F has made things smoother: a jump has become a joint."},
 {"u":"16a5.1","q":"Why is “f jumps at c, so F jumps at c” a move to watch out for?","a":"area-never-jumps: the area function is continuous wherever f has an area, since a bounded height adds only a small area over a narrow piece","w":["F jumps twice as far as f","F has no value where f jumps","f cannot jump if it is integrable"],"why":"|F(y) − F(x)| ≤ M|y − x|. The step's area function has a joint at the jump, not a jump."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16b1.1","q":"What does the area-slope technique say, and what is the plain reading of (F(c + h) − F(c))/h?","a":"where f is continuous at c, the area function has a slope there and F′(c) = f(c); the quotient is the average height of f between c and c + h, which is held near f(c) when h is small","w":["F′(c) = f′(c) wherever f is integrable; the quotient is the slope of f","F(c) = f(c) wherever f is continuous; the quotient is the area up to c","F′(c) = 0 at every c; the quotient is the width h"],"why":"F(c + h) − F(c) is the area over a strip of width h. Continuity at c keeps f within ε/2 of f(c) on the strip."},
 {"u":"16b2.1","q":"What does the antiderivative-exists technique say, and what did rung 14 lack that it supplies?","a":"for f continuous on an interval, the area function from any point of it is an antiderivative of f; rung 14 could only say that any two antiderivatives differ by a constant, not that there is one","w":["every function has exactly one antiderivative; rung 14 lacked the constant","an antiderivative exists only for polynomials; rung 14 lacked the power technique","the area function is the slope of f; rung 14 lacked slopes"],"why":"Continuous-is-integrable gives the area function, and area-slope gives its slope at every input. It may not be a function anyone can write with powers and roots."},
 {"u":"16b3.1","q":"What does the moving-end technique say, and what is the slope of ∫ₓ^q f(t) dt?","a":"the slope of ∫ₚ^{g(x)} f(t) dt is f(g(x)) · g′(x), by chain for slopes with the area function outside; with x at the lower end the slope is −f(x)","w":["the slope is f(g(x)), with no factor; with x at the lower end it is f(x)","the slope is f′(g(x)); with x at the lower end it is f(q)","the slope is g′(x) alone; with x at the lower end it is 0"],"why":"(d/dx) ∫₀^{x³} √(1 + t²) dt = √(1 + x⁶) · 3x². Swapping the ends changes the sign, by 15e's convention."},
 {"u":"16b4.1","q":"In which two ways can F′(c) = f(c) fail where f is not continuous at c?","a":"at a jump the area function has a joint and no slope; at a single point raised off the graph the area function has a slope, and it is not the raised value","w":["F can jump at c, or F can be unbounded there","F′(c) can be twice f(c), or half of it","F can have no value at c, or two values"],"why":"For the step, the slopes from the two sides are 0 and 1. For one raised point, F = 0 everywhere, so F′(0) = 0 ≠ 1. Area-slope promises nothing at either."},
 {"u":"16b5.1","q":"Why is “(d/dx) ∫₀^{x²} f(t) dt = f(x²)” a move to watch out for?","a":"the upper end is an inside, and its slope 2x is missing: moving-end gives f(x²) · 2x","w":["the answer should be f(x)","the answer should be f(t)","the answer should be 2f(x)"],"why":"The integral is the area function with x² put in, a chain. Chain for slopes multiplies by the inside's slope."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16c1.1","q":"What does the area-is-a-change technique say, and what does G(x)|ₚ^q mean?","a":"for f continuous on [p, q] and any antiderivative G of f there, ∫ₚ^q f(x) dx = G(q) − G(p); the bar with its ends means G(q) − G(p)","w":["∫ₚ^q f = G(p) − G(q); the bar means G(p) · G(q)","∫ₚ^q f = G′(q); the bar means the slope of G","∫ₚ^q f = G(q) for one special G only; the bar means G at the middle"],"why":"The area function is an antiderivative, so it is G + C, and it is 0 at p, which makes C = −G(p). Any antiderivative serves, since the C cancels."},
 {"u":"16c2.1","q":"What does the change-for-any-integrable technique say, which hypothesis of area-is-a-change does it drop, and what does it ask instead?","a":"if f is integrable on [p, q] and G is continuous there with G′ = f inside, then ∫ₚ^q f = G(q) − G(p); it drops the continuity of f, and asks that f be integrable and G continuous","w":["it drops the antiderivative; it asks that f be a polynomial","it drops integrability; it asks that f be bounded","it drops nothing; it asks that G be constant"],"why":"Average-slope on each piece of any partition gives G(xᵢ) − G(xᵢ₋₁) = f(tᵢ)Δxᵢ; the rises collapse to G(q) − G(p), which sits between every L and U."},
 {"u":"16c3.1","q":"What is the difference between ∫ₚ^q f(x) dx and ∫ f(x) dx?","a":"the first is one number; the second, with no ends, is the whole family of antiderivatives G + C","w":["they are the same thing written two ways","the first is a function and the second a number","the first is a family and the second its largest member"],"why":"Area-is-a-change is the bridge: pick any member G of the family and the number is G(q) − G(p)."},
 {"u":"16c4.1","q":"What two moves does every line of this footing make, and what does the inverse-square-area technique say?","a":"name a function with the right slope, then subtract its values at the ends; for 0 < p < q, ∫ₚ^q dx/x² = 1/p − 1/q","w":["cut into equal pieces, then add the boxes; the area under 1/x² is 0","take the slope of f, then add C; ∫ₚ^q dx/x² = 1/q − 1/p","find the greatest value, then multiply by the width; ∫ₚ^q dx/x² = q − p"],"why":"(−1/x)′ = 1/x², so the area is (−1/q) − (−1/p). The hard part has moved from a sum to finding G."},
 {"u":"16c5.1","q":"Why is “∫₋₁¹ dx/x² = −1/x|₋₁¹ = −2” a move to watch out for?","a":"1/x² is not bounded on [−1, 1], so it has no integral there, and −1/x has no value at 0; the hypotheses fail","w":["the subtraction is in the wrong order, and the area is 2","−1/x is not an antiderivative of 1/x² anywhere","the answer should be 0 by odd-and-even-areas"],"why":"A positive function with a negative area is the warning. Area-is-a-change needs f continuous on the whole closed interval."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16d1.1","q":"What does the substitution technique for areas say, and which technique for slopes is it read backwards?","a":"∫ᵥʷ f(g(x)) g′(x) dx = ∫ from g(v) to g(w) of f(r) dr, for g with a continuous slope and f continuous on g's values; it is chain for slopes read backwards","w":["∫ f(g(x)) dx = ∫ f(r) dr with the same ends; it is product for slopes backwards","∫ f(x) g′(x) dx = f(w) − f(v); it is quotient for slopes backwards","∫ f(g(x)) g′(x) dx = f(g(w)); it is power for slopes backwards"],"why":"With F an antiderivative of f, (F ∘ g)′ = f(g(x)) · g′(x). Both sides are F(g(w)) − F(g(v)) by area-is-a-change."},
 {"u":"16d2.1","q":"What does the line r = g(x), dr = g′(x) dx say, and what is it not?","a":"that wherever g′(x) dx sits next to a function of g(x) it may be traded for dr, with the ends traded for the values of r; it is a reminder of substitution for areas, not a statement about numbers","w":["that dr is a small number equal to g′(x) times dx; it is not a reminder","that r and x are the same letter; it is not about ends","that g′(x) may be dropped; it is not about g"],"why":"No number dr or dx has been defined. The trade always gives the right answer under the technique's hypotheses, which is why the marks have lasted."},
 {"u":"16d3.1","q":"What are an even function and an odd function, and what does odd-and-even-areas say?","a":"even means f(−x) = f(x) and odd means f(−x) = −f(x); for f continuous on [−p, p], the integral over it is 0 when f is odd and twice the integral over [0, p] when f is even","w":["even means f(2x) = f(x) and odd means f is never 0; the integral is always 0","even means f ≥ 0 and odd means f ≤ 0; the integral doubles for odd f","even means the degree is 2 and odd the degree is 1; the integral over [−p, p] is p"],"why":"The names come from the powers: x² and x⁴ are even, x and x³ odd."},
 {"u":"16d4.1","q":"Which substitution turns ∫₋ₚ⁰ f into an integral over [0, p], and what does it give for odd and for even f?","a":"r = −x, which gives ∫₀ᵖ f(−r) dr; for odd f that is the negative of ∫₀ᵖ f, so the halves cancel, and for even f it equals ∫₀ᵖ f, so they double","w":["r = x + p; the halves add for odd f and cancel for even f","r = x²; both halves are 0","r = 1/x; the halves are equal for every f"],"why":"dr = −dx and the ends p and 0 come in the other order; the convention turns the sign back. Then split-in-any-order joins the halves."},
 {"u":"16d5.1","q":"Why is “∫₀¹ 2x(x² + 1)³ dx = ∫₀¹ r³ dr” a move to watch out for?","a":"the ends 0 and 1 belong to x; for r they are g(0) = 1 and g(1) = 2","w":["the 2x should stay in the integral","r³ should be r⁴","the ends should both be 0"],"why":"∫₁² r³ dr = 15/4. Trading the letter trades the ends too."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16e1.1","q":"What does the parts technique for areas say, and which technique for slopes is it read backwards?","a":"∫ₚ^q f g′ = f g taken between the ends, less ∫ₚ^q f′ g; it is product for slopes read backwards","w":["∫ₚ^q f g′ = ∫ f · ∫ g′; it is chain for slopes backwards","∫ₚ^q f g′ = f g less ∫ f′ g′; it is quotient for slopes backwards","∫ₚ^q f g′ = f′ g between the ends; it is power for slopes backwards"],"why":"(fg)′ = f′g + fg′, so area-is-a-change gives ∫(f′g + fg′) = fg between the ends, and areas-add splits the left side."},
 {"u":"16e2.1","q":"How are f and g′ chosen in parts, and why is a power of x the usual f?","a":"so that the leftover integral ∫ f′g is simpler than the one started with: f gets simpler when its slope is taken and g′ is easy to undo; each slope lowers a power of x by one","w":["at random, since every choice is equally good; a power of x is the usual g′","so that f′ = g′; a power of x has no slope","so that the bar term is 0; a power of x is always 0 at the ends"],"why":"Parts is never wrong; it can be useless. For ∫₀¹ x(1 − x)⁴ dx, f = x gives 1/30 at once, and the other choice gives a harder integral."},
 {"u":"16e3.1","q":"What does the areas-under-xᵐ(1 − x)ⁿ technique say, and how does its proof run?","a":"∫₀¹ xᵐ(1 − x)ⁿ dx = m! n!/(m + n + 1)!; by induction on n for every m at once, with parts moving one power from (1 − x) across to x, the bar term being 0 at both ends","w":["the area is 1/(m + n); by expanding (1 − x)ⁿ in full","the area is m · n; by substitution r = 1 − x alone","the area is 1/(m! n!); by equal pieces and a squeeze"],"why":"Parts gives I(m, n) = (n/(m + 1)) · I(m + 1, n − 1), and the base I(m, 0) = 1/(m + 1) is the power technique backwards."},
 {"u":"16e4.1","q":"What has the area under x²(1 − x)³ on [0, 1] become, in rung 4's terms?","a":"a count: it is 2! 3!/6! = 1/60, and (m + n + 1) times the area is one over the number of ways to choose m things from m + n","w":["a slope: it is the slope of x³ at 1","a sum of six boxes of equal width","a product of two areas, ⅓ · ¼"],"why":"No expanding is needed. 6 · (1/60) = 1/10, and there are 10 ways to choose 2 things from 5."},
 {"u":"16e5.1","q":"Why is “∫ f(x)g(x) dx = ∫ f · ∫ g” a move to watch out for?","a":"no product technique for areas says that: ∫₀¹ x · x dx = ⅓, while the two integrals multiplied give ¼; parts is what the product technique gives","w":["products can never be integrated","the right side should be ∫ f + ∫ g","it is true whenever f = g"],"why":"The slope of a product is not the product of slopes, and its reverse fails the same way."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16f1.1","q":"What does antiderivatives-add-and-scale say, what is checking by differentiation, and why does it never fail?","a":"antiderivatives of f and g add to one of f + g, and a constant times one is one of the constant times f; to check an answer take its slope and compare with the integrand; taking slopes is a fixed procedure, and finding antiderivatives is not","w":["antiderivatives multiply; checking is integrating twice; it never fails because C cancels","an antiderivative of f + g is the larger of the two; checking is putting in x = 0; it fails for polynomials","antiderivatives of f and g are equal; checking is comparing ends; it never fails because areas are positive"],"why":"(F + G)′ = F′ + G′ by slopes-add and (kF)′ = kF′ by constant-factor for slopes. The table is the slope techniques read backwards."},
 {"u":"16f2.1","q":"What is an initial value problem, and why does it have one answer on an interval?","a":"a demand G′ = f on an interval together with one value of G; all-antiderivatives says every answer is one antiderivative plus C, and the one value pins C","w":["a demand that G(0) = 0; it has one answer because f is 0","a slope with no condition; it has endlessly many answers","two values of G with no slope; it has one answer by same-ratio"],"why":"G′ = 3x² − 2 with G(1) = 5 gives x³ − 2x + C, and 5 = 1 − 2 + C makes C = 6."},
 {"u":"16f3.1","q":"Why does “∫ x⁻² dx = −1/x + C” need a C of its own on each side of 0?","a":"all-antiderivatives is about one interval, and the domain of x⁻² is two: −1/x on the left and −1/x + 1 on the right has slope x⁻² everywhere and is not −1/x plus one constant","w":["−1/x is not an antiderivative on the left","the two sides have different slopes","C must be 0 on the left"],"why":"Most books write one C and leave the interval to the reader."},
 {"u":"16f4.1","q":"What does the no-power-for-1/x technique say, and what does 1/x have all the same?","a":"no real c and rational r make c · xʳ an antiderivative of 1/x on x > 0; yet 1/x is continuous there, so it has an antiderivative: its area function from 1","w":["1/x has the antiderivative x⁰/0; it has no area function","1/x has no antiderivative at all; it has only a slope","1/x has antiderivative −1/x²; it has a table entry"],"why":"c r xʳ = 1 for every x > 0 forces xʳ = 1 everywhere, so r = 0, and then c r = 0. The function waits for rung 17 to be named."},
 {"u":"16f5.1","q":"Why is “∫ (x² + 1)² dx = (x² + 1)³/3 + C” a move to watch out for?","a":"check by the slope: it is 2x(x² + 1)², not (x² + 1)², since no factor 2x is there for the inside; expand first","w":["the 3 should be a 2","the power should stay 2","the answer needs no C"],"why":"The power line is for a power of x, or of a straight inside. Checking by differentiation catches the missing factor."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16g1.1","q":"What does the area-between technique say, and what is done where the graphs cross?","a":"where f ≥ g on [p, q], the area of the region between them is ∫ₚ^q (f − g), top minus bottom; where they cross, cut the interval at the crossings and take top minus bottom on each piece","w":["the area is ∫ f · ∫ g; crossings are ignored","the area is ∫ (f + g); at a crossing the sign is kept","the area is the size of ∫ (f − g) over the whole interval; nothing is cut"],"why":"Boxes from the top of g to the bottom of f fit inside, and boxes from the bottom of g to the top of f cover it. The squeeze closes on ∫(f − g)."},
 {"u":"16g2.1","q":"What changes in area-between when the region is sliced across y instead of x?","a":"the boxes lie on their sides, and the integrand is right edge minus left edge, as functions of y, between the heights where the edges meet","w":["nothing can be done: areas are sliced in x only","the integrand is top minus bottom, with dy written for dx","the area is doubled"],"why":"Between x = y² and x = y + 2, ∫₋₁² (y + 2 − y²) dy = 9/2. The upright road needs two integrals and gives the same number."},
 {"u":"16g3.1","q":"How are the rising, the falling, the crests and the bends of an area function read off f?","a":"F rises where f > 0 and falls where f < 0; F has a crest where f changes from plus to minus, and a trough the other way; F bends up where f is increasing and down where f is decreasing","w":["F rises where f is increasing; F has a crest where f has one","F rises where f is large; F bends up where f > 0","F falls where f is decreasing; F has a crest where f is 0 on both sides"],"why":"F′ = f by area-slope, so rung 14's slope-sign-order, first-test and bends-up read F off f."},
 {"u":"16g4.1","q":"What are the net area and the total area of f on [p, q], and how is the total found?","a":"the net is ∫ₚ^q f and the total is ∫ₚ^q |f|; find the crossings, integrate on each piece between them, and add the sizes","w":["the net is ∫ |f| and the total ∫ f; the total is the size of the net","both are ∫ f; the total is found by doubling","the net is the area above the axis only; the total is found by squaring f"],"why":"For x² − 1 on [0, 2] the pieces are −⅔ and 4/3: net ⅔, total 2. Between crossings a continuous f keeps one sign."},
 {"u":"16g5.1","q":"What does the average height of f on [p, q] give when multiplied by the width, and what did 15f and 16c each add about it?","a":"the area: the box of that height on [p, q] has the same area as the region under f; 15f proved such a height is reached somewhere, and 16c finds its value","w":["the slope of f; 15f found its value and 16c proved it exists","the greatest value of f; 15f and 16c both bound it","the width again; 15f named it and 16c drew it"],"why":"The average of x² on [0, 3] is ⅓ · 9 = 3, reached at √3."},
 {"u":"16g6.1","q":"What did the Paris teacher's figure say about a body whose speed grows evenly from nothing?","a":"the whole distance is the same as if the body had moved all the time at the speed it had at the middle instant","w":["the whole distance is the final speed times the time","the body covers equal distances in equal times","the distance grows with the cube of the time"],"why":"The speeds stand as heights on a line of times, cut off by a straight line. The area under it is the middle height times the width."},
 {"u":"16g7.1","q":"Why is “the area between x and x² on [0, 2] is ∫₀² (x − x²) dx” a move to watch out for?","a":"the graphs cross at 1: x is on top on [0, 1] and x² on [1, 2], so the interval must be cut there and the difference swapped","w":["x² is on top on the whole of [0, 2]","the graphs never meet on [0, 2]","the integrand should be x · x²"],"why":"Uncut, the integral is a net, with the second piece counted against the first. Top minus bottom on each piece gives the area."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16h1.1","q":"Which sign in an integrand calls for substitution, and which calls for parts?","a":"an inside g(x) with g′(x) as a factor, up to a constant, or a straight inside, calls for substitution; a power of x times something easy to undo calls for parts","w":["any product calls for substitution; any fraction calls for parts","a sum calls for substitution; a constant factor calls for parts","a power of x alone calls for substitution; a straight inside calls for parts"],"why":"2x next to x² + 1 is the first sign. With parts, each slope lowers the power by one. A fraction of polynomials is divided first."},
 {"u":"16h2.1","q":"When a substitution leaves an x over, what is done with it, and what happens to the ends?","a":"the leftover x is written in terms of r, as x = r − 1 when r = x + 1; on a definite integral the ends are traded for the values of r there","w":["the leftover x is treated as a constant; the ends stay as they are","the leftover x is dropped; the ends are doubled","the leftover x is moved outside the integral; the ends become 0 and 1"],"why":"∫ x/√(x + 1) dx becomes ∫ (r − 1) r^(−1/2) dr, which splits into powers. One letter only may stand inside the integral."},
 {"u":"16h3.1","q":"What does completing the square do for ∫ (x − 1)/(x² − 2x + 5)² dx, and where does it stop helping?","a":"it shows the bottom's inside as (x − 1)² + 4, whose slope 2(x − 1) is the factor on top, so substitution finishes it; with no factor x − 1 on top, as in ∫ dx/((x − 1)² + 4), no table entry fits","w":["it turns the integrand into a polynomial; it always finishes the job","it removes the square in the bottom; it stops helping when the top is x − 1","it gives the answer directly with no substitution; it never fails"],"why":"The answer is −1/(2(x² − 2x + 5)) + C. The other integral's area function exists by antiderivative-exists, though the table cannot write it."},
 {"u":"16h4.1","q":"What does the areas-under-(1 − x²)ⁿ technique say, and how does Jₙ come out of a line with Jₙ on both sides?","a":"Jₙ = (2n/(2n + 1)) · Jₙ₋₁ with J₀ = 1; parts gives Jₙ = 2n(Jₙ₋₁ − Jₙ), and collecting Jₙ on one side gives (2n + 1)Jₙ = 2nJₙ₋₁","w":["Jₙ = Jₙ₋₁ for every n; the two Jₙ cancel","Jₙ = 0 for every n; Jₙ is taken away from one side","Jₙ = 2n · Jₙ₋₁; the Jₙ on the right is dropped"],"why":"With f = (1 − x²)ⁿ and g′ = 1, the leftover holds x², rewritten as 1 − (1 − x²)."},
 {"u":"16h5.1","q":"What is a reduction formula, and why is the step with Jₙ on both sides not circular?","a":"a line giving one integral of a family through a smaller one; the line with Jₙ on both sides is an equation for Jₙ, and one line of algebra solves it","w":["a formula that makes every integral 0; the step is circular and is allowed anyway","a table of integrals; the step uses Jₙ to define Jₙ","a substitution with new ends; the step is avoided"],"why":"J₁ = 2/3, J₂ = 8/15, J₃ = 16/35: each a product of even numbers over odd ones."},
 {"u":"16h6.1","q":"Why is “Jₙ = 2n(Jₙ₋₁ − Jₙ), so Jₙ = 0” a move to watch out for?","a":"Jₙ cannot be cancelled from one side only; collect it: (2n + 1)Jₙ = 2nJₙ₋₁","w":["Jₙ₋₁ is 0, so Jₙ is too","the line should read Jₙ = 2n · Jₙ","Jₙ is negative, not 0"],"why":"Jₙ is an area under a positive function on [0, 1], so it cannot be 0. The same change must be made to both sides."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16i1.1","q":"What does ∫ₐ^∞ f(x) dx mean, and what does ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx mean when f has a bad end at a?","a":"the limit of ∫ₐᵇ f as b grows without bound, when it exists; and the limit of the integral from c to b as c comes down to a from the right, when it exists","w":["the integral with ∞ put in for b; and the integral with a put in directly","the sum of f at the whole numbers; and f(a) times b − a","always a number; and always 0"],"why":"∫₁^∞ x⁻² dx = 1 and ∫₀¹ x^(−1/2) dx = 2. A region that never ends can have area 1, and one that climbs past every height can have area 2."},
 {"u":"16i2.1","q":"What do the out-to-forever and in-to-0 techniques say about x⁻ᵖ, and why does ∫₁^∞ dx/x diverge?","a":"out to forever from 1 it converges exactly when p > 1, and in to 0 from 1 exactly when p < 1; for 1/x the area up to n + 1 is at least ½ + ⅓ + ⋯ + 1/(n + 1), which passes every bound","w":["both converge for every p; 1/x diverges because it is not continuous","out to forever converges when p < 1 and in to 0 when p > 1; 1/x converges to 1","both diverge for every p; 1/x diverges because 1/x does not head to 0"],"why":"Grouping by doubling shows 1 + ½ + ⋯ + 1/2ᵐ ≥ 1 + m/2. p = 1 is the edge on both sides, and it fails both."},
 {"u":"16i3.1","q":"What does the comparison technique for areas say, and where does its proof use completeness?","a":"if 0 ≤ f ≤ g and the integral of g out to forever converges, so does that of f, and it is no larger; the area function of f is increasing and bounded above, and completeness gives its values a sup, which is the limit","w":["if f ≤ g and ∫ f converges then ∫ g converges; completeness gives g a greatest value","if f ≥ g ≥ 0 and ∫ g converges then ∫ f converges; completeness is not used","if f and g are continuous then both converge; completeness gives a partition"],"why":"An increasing function bounded above heads to its sup. The value of the limit may not be in the table; its existence is what is proved."},
 {"u":"16i4.1","q":"Why is “∫₁^∞ dx/x converges, since 1/x → 0” a move to watch out for?","a":"heights shrinking to 0 are not enough: out-to-forever says the area under 1/x passes every bound","w":["1/x does not head to 0","the integral converges to 1","1/x is not integrable on any [1, b]"],"why":"x⁻ᵖ heads to 0 for every p > 0, and its area out to forever converges only for p > 1."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16j1.1","q":"What are the three volume axioms, and what is the slice of S at x = k?","a":"a box has volume the product of its three sides, a solid inside another has no more volume, and two solids sharing only points on one flat wall have volumes that add; the slice is the set of pairs (y, z) with (k, y, z) in S","w":["volume is area times area, every solid has a volume, and volumes multiply; the slice is the point (k, 0, 0)","a box has volume the sum of its sides, and volumes never add; the slice is the solid left of x = k","volume is a slope, a limit and a sum; the slice is the number k"],"why":"Each is earned as the area axioms were. Nothing more about a solid is used than its slices."},
 {"u":"16j2.1","q":"What does the cross-sections technique say, and what does it mean for slices to be tight?","a":"a solid's volume is the integral of the areas of its cross sections; tight means each piece of a fine partition has an inner region inside every slice over it and an outer one holding them all, with the total of their gap times the widths as small as you like","w":["the volume is the largest slice times the length; tight means all slices are equal","the volume is the sum of the slice areas; tight means the slices are boxes","the volume is the area of one slice; tight means the solid has no holes"],"why":"Slabs on the inner regions lie inside the solid and slabs on the outer ones cover it. A slab on a region of area A and length q − p has volume A(q − p)."},
 {"u":"16j3.1","q":"What do the prism, pyramid and matching-slices techniques say?","a":"equal slices of area B over a length h give volume Bh; slices shrinking as the square of x/h give Bh/3; and two solids whose slices at each x have the same area have the same volume","w":["a prism has volume Bh/2; a pyramid Bh/2; matching slices give different volumes when one solid leans","a prism has volume B + h; a pyramid B · h; matching slices must have the same shape","a prism has volume Bh; a pyramid Bh/2; matching slices give equal volumes only for boxes"],"why":"∫₀ʰ B dx = Bh and ∫₀ʰ (B/h²)x² dx = Bh/3. A leaning pile has the same volume as a straight one."},
 {"u":"16j4.1","q":"How is the slice area found when a solid stands on the region between two graphs with square slices?","a":"the side of the square at x is the gap between the graphs, top minus bottom, and the slice area is its square","w":["the slice area is the gap itself","the slice area is the sum of the two heights","the slice area is the gap times x"],"why":"Between x² and √x the side is √x − x², the area (√x − x²)² = x − 2x^(5/2) + x⁴, and the volume 9/70."},
 {"u":"16j5.1","q":"What is density?","a":"a solid's mass per unit volume, m/V when it is the same throughout, in kg/m³","w":["a solid's volume per unit mass","a solid's mass times its volume","the area of a solid's slice"],"why":"It is a rate of mass against volume. Where it changes through the solid, one quotient no longer serves."},
 {"u":"16j6.1","q":"What does the density technique say when the density depends on x?","a":"the mass is the integral of the density times the slice area, ∫ᵤᵛ ρ(x)A(x) dx","w":["the mass is the average density plus the volume","the mass is ρ(u) times the volume","the mass is the integral of ρ(x) alone, whatever the slices"],"why":"On a thin slab the mass lies between the least and greatest of ρA times the width. For the core, ∫₀³ 2(1200 + 300x) dx = 9900 kg."},
 {"u":"16j7.1","q":"Why is “the volume of a pyramid is ½Bh” a move to watch out for?","a":"the slice area shrinks as the square of the distance from the tip, not in proportion to it, so the integral gives a third","w":["the slice area does not shrink at all","a half is right for a pyramid on a square base","the volume is Bh, with no fraction"],"why":"A half is the triangle's share of a box, where the width shrinks once. Here both directions shrink, and ∫₀ʰ x² dx brings the 3."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16·cb12"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16·cb12"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16·cb12.1.1","q":"What does pressure-with-depth say, and how does a still column prove it?","a":"in a still liquid of density ρ the pressure at depth h is p₀ + ρgh; a column from the surface down stays still, so the push up on its base equals the push down on its top plus its weight ρAhg","w":["the pressure at depth h is p₀ times ρgh; the column's weight is multiplied by the surface pressure","the pressure is p₀ at every depth; a still column feels no push at its base","the pressure at depth h is ρgh/A; a wider column is pressed harder at its base"],"why":"The column is a prism of volume Ah. Balance gives pA = p₀A + ρAhg, and the area A cancels."},
 {"u":"16·cb12.2.1","q":"What does the buoyancy technique say, and why does the depth not matter?","a":"a body wholly under a still liquid is pushed up with ρgV, the weight of the liquid it displaces; the push up on the bottom less the push down on the top keeps only the difference in depth, so the depth itself cancels","w":["the push up is ρgV times the depth; a deeper body is pushed up harder","the push up equals the body's own weight; the depth cancels because the body is still","the push up is ρgh on the top face alone; the bottom face does not count"],"why":"For a box of height H with its top at depth d, ρg(d + H)A − ρgdA = ρgHA = ρgV. The d drops out in the subtraction."},
 {"u":"16·cb12.3.1","q":"What does the floating technique say, and why must a settling depth exist?","a":"a body settles where the submerged volume V_s has ρgV_s = mg; if its density is less than the liquid's, the push up runs continuously from 0 to more than its weight, so in-between-value gives a depth where they are equal","w":["a body settles where its whole volume is under; a depth exists because every body sinks a little","a body settles where ρgV_s = mg; a depth exists only when the body is denser than the liquid","a body settles where V_s equals m; a depth exists because the liquid is still"],"why":"The share under is (m/V)/ρ. Ice in sea water floats with about 89% under."},
 {"u":"16·cb12.4.1","q":"Why is “heavy things sink” a move to watch out for?","a":"what decides is density against the liquid's, not weight: a body sinks when its mass per unit volume is greater than ρ","w":["nothing heavy can be pushed up by a liquid","weight decides, but only below a certain depth","light things sink too, once they are wholly under"],"why":"Dense things sink. A steel ship is heavy and floats, because its volume includes the air inside."});
